{"chapter": "categories", "node_kind": "paragraph", "parent_tag": "0029", "placeholders": {"MATH_0001": "$2$", "STRUCT_0001": "\\noindent"}, "render": {"prefix": "", "suffix": ""}, "risk_level": "R2", "schema_version": 1, "source_commit": "a04446e57ec1fbc252a871afcec7752fb2807b14", "source_math_hash": "sha256:b15315ec3c2fa7b154c0d17c24b63d47f12b572ea3fa602a460510e1a685b882", "source_status": "CURRENT", "source_structure_hash": "sha256:f81b53a5ba5f9b11123fafe7cfd9f6fcbf92d34908a697ee31f56c78e4e42e56", "source_text": "<STRUCT_0001>\nAs in the case of the pushouts above, coequalizers when\nthey exist are unique up to unique isomorphism, and this follows\nfrom the uniqueness of equalizers upon considering the opposite\ncategory. There is a straightforward generalization of this definition\nto the case where we have more than <MATH_0001> morphisms.", "source_text_hash": "sha256:b51b37ece74f3f056d3d3746231c087408b78a40d19308d2d45d8c1b9f88ff79", "unit_id": "tag:0029:paragraph-0001"}
